<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The published contract between services: integration event records, and
    nothing else.

    This is the ONE thing services share, and sharing it is a considered
    trade-off rather than an oversight. The alternative - every service
    hand-copying the record it consumes - means a field added to OrderPaid is a
    silent mismatch nobody notices until a null appears in production. Sharing
    the record makes that a compile error.

    What keeps it safe is the strict rule on what may live here:

      * records with primitive and primitive-collection properties, nothing else
      * NO behaviour, NO domain types, NO validation, NO references to any service
      * additive changes only - a removed or renamed property breaks every
        consumer that has not redeployed yet

    The moment a domain type leaks in, this stops being a contract and becomes a
    shared model, and the services stop being independently deployable. That is
    the failure mode this comment exists to prevent.

    See docs/adr/0019-shared-integration-event-contracts.md.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\building-blocks\EventBus\ECommerce.EventBus.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
